import Native

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The format primitive, and where a formatted string goes - Implementation-Storage.md parts 8 and 9.
   ==========================================================================================

   `Show.yana` beside this is what a *value* contributes to a format. This is the other half: what a
   *hole* contributes to the size, which is the one line that makes part 8's three strategies one
   shape, and the write that the whole scheme exists to make free.

   Two functions, and that is the honest measure of the design: everything else about `"x = {x}"` is
   the compiler expanding the literal into measure-size-format-write over the `Show` instances, so
   there is nothing here for it to call into but these.
-}

{-
   What one hole contributes to a format's size - Implementation-Storage.md part 8, step 2.

   A plain function rather than something the compiler emits inline, because it is the same three
   lines at every hole and because this is where `Nothing` becomes a *number*. Strategy (c) - the
   unbounded case - is not a third code path in the formatter: it is this answering zero, so the
   buffer is seeded with the bounds that are known and the appends grow past it. That the three
   strategies are one shape is the whole of part 8's argument, and this is the line that makes it
   true.

   Zero and not one, and not a guess at a typical size. A seed is a promise about nothing: too small
   costs one growth, and inventing a number here would cost every bounded format the difference.
-}
fn formatBound(bound: Maybe(Size)) -> Size = match bound:
    Just(units) -> units
    Nothing -> 0

{-
   `print` - Implementation-Storage.md part 9.

   The point of the whole scheme, stated as one function: `print("x = {x}\n")` measures, sizes a
   buffer, formats into it and writes it once. Under strategy (a) the buffer never existed anywhere
   but the frame, so the first-day program's output path is one `write` and no allocation at all.

   Natively this is `writeFile` on the standard output descriptor, handed the run's base and the
   count - which is exactly what part 9 says, and is possible only because a growable string *is* a
   run of units rather than something that has to be flattened first. The result is discarded: what a
   short write or a closed pipe should do is a question about a `Result` type this does not have yet,
   and inventing an answer here would be the wrong place to make that decision.

   On JS it is `console.log`, which is not the same operation and is the honest mapping rather than a
   compromise: the host has no file descriptors, and its line-oriented console is what a program's
   output goes to. The trailing newline a caller writes is therefore doubled there - `console.log`
   adds its own - which is a real behavioural difference between the targets and is the reason this
   is `print` rather than `write`.
-}
@platform(native) pub fn print(text: String):
    -- The units as bytes, which is what a descriptor writes and what `CodeUnit` stops being able to
    -- say on its own - see `Native/Text.native.yana`. Natively the two are one width and the
    -- reinterpretation is free; what it buys is that the string's own type keeps saying "encoding
    -- unit" everywhere else.
    let _ = writeStandardOutput(bitcast(units(text)) :: %U8, length(text))

@platform(js) pub fn print(text: String): hostLog(text)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   A recipe, and the value it is applied to - the shape every formatter in this library has.
   ==========================================================================================

   A formatter used to be a record per rendering: `NumberFormat(a)` held a reference to a number and
   the five settings to write it under, `DateFormat` held a `Date` and nine, and each one restated
   its settings a second time as the parameters of the reader beside it. Nine fields with defaults in
   `DateFormat` and the same nine as `readDate`'s arguments is one fact written twice, and the promise
   that a round trip through the pair is the identity had nothing enforcing it - `iso8601Compact` and
   `readIso8601Compact` each named `dateSeparator: 0` on their own.

   So the settings are a record of their own - a *style* - and this is the one wrapper that puts a
   style back together with a subject. `Formatted(DateStyle, Date)` is what `DateFormat` was, and the
   nine settings exist once, in `DateStyle`, where `readWith` reads them too.

   **`showWith` takes the whole wrapper rather than a style and a subject.** A member declared
   `fn showWith(style: s, value: 'a, &to: String)` cannot be implemented at a subject that is a
   window: at `a = Flat(U8)` - which is what a hex dump formats - the class's `'a` and the instance's
   own spelling of it do not match, in either direction. Taking `Formatted(s, a)` substitutes the
   record instead of the loan and the instance names a concrete type.

   **Two classes and not one.** `padded`, `fixed` and `duration` have no settings-taking reader and
   should not have to implement a `readWith` they would answer wrongly; a class that carried both
   directions would make three formatters lie to satisfy it.
-}
pub data Formatted(s, a) {value: 'a, style: s}

pub class ShowWith(s, a):
  fn showWith(subject: Formatted(s, a), &to: String) -> {}
  fn showBoundWith(subject: Formatted(s, a)) -> Maybe(Size) = Nothing

{-
   The reading direction, keyed on the same style type.

   The style is a value and not a wrapper, because a read has no subject yet - which is the
   asymmetry that kept the readers free functions before. `Read(a)`'s own member carries no settings
   and cannot; this is where a radix, a separator or a date order is asked for, and the instance is
   selected by the expected type exactly as `Read`'s is.
-}
pub class ReadWith(s, a):
  fn readWith(style: s, from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, a)

-- The bridge, and the whole reason a formatter is still an ordinary format hole: anything with a
-- `ShowWith` instance is shown by wrapping it, so `showBound` still sizes the buffer and the
-- settings still fold. A style is a record of scalars, so `TrivialCopy` is what lets one be copied
-- into the wrapper rather than borrowed out of it.
instance (ShowWith(s, a), TrivialCopy(s)) Show(Formatted(s, a)):
    fn show(value: Formatted(s, a), &to: String) = showWith(value, to)
    fn showBound(value: Formatted(s, a)) -> Maybe(Size) = showBoundWith(value)

{-
   Applying a style a caller is holding - `with(n, wide)` where `wide` is a style bound above.

   **The style is `'s` and that is what keeps this out of a constructor.** Provenance is
   field-insensitive and cannot see that a type variable holds no references - `TrivialCopy` does not
   say so either, a window being trivially copyable and a reference both - so an aggregate argument
   reaching a record that holds one is read as a root of it. A caller passing a style it owns is fine;
   a *constructor* building its own style and handing the result back would be returning a root into
   its own frame, and is refused.

   Which is why a named formatter writes the record literal itself - `Formatted {value: value, style:
   httpStyle()}`. At a concrete style type the compiler can see the style holds no references, the
   copy is not a reference flow, and there is no loan at all. See `Date/Format.yana`, where every
   constructor is written that way.
-}
pub fn (ShowWith(s, a), TrivialCopy(s)) with(value: 'a, style: 's) -> Formatted(s, a) =
    Formatted {value: value, style: style}

{-
   A whole string as one value, under a style - `readWith`'s cursor form with the cursor's job done.

   Every reader in this library advances a cursor and stops where the value ends, which is what a
   parser wants and is not what a caller holding one string wants: `bytesOfHex(text)` and its like
   existed per format to say "and there had better be nothing left". One function says it for all of
   them, because "did the cursor reach the end" is not a question about hexadecimal.

   A trailing unit is `NoDigits` rather than a shorter success, on `Core/Read.yana`'s terms: what did
   not parse did not parse, and a caller who wants the prefix asks `readWith` for it.
-}
pub fn (ReadWith(s, a)) readAll(style: s, text: String) -> Result(ReadError, a):
    let &at = 0 :: Size

    -- Ascribed, because `a` appears in this signature's result and in no argument: the class has no
    -- dependency from `s` to `a` - one style reads at twelve types in `Format/Number.yana` - so the
    -- inner call is told which instance it is by the same expected type that tells the outer one.
    let outcome = readWith(style, text, at) :: Result(ReadError, a)
    let ->value = outcome?

    if at != length(text) then return Err(NoDigits)
    return Ok(value)
